1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:03,040
Hi,  and  welcome  back  to  Act.  So

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:04,759
far,  most  of  the  attention  has

3
00:00:04,759 --> 00:00:07,200
been  on  understanding  yourself.  Now

4
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:09,559
the  view  starts  to  move  outward.

5
00:00:09,559 --> 00:00:10,920
Every  day,  you  move  through

6
00:00:10,920 --> 00:00:12,799
different  parts  of  life,  and  in

7
00:00:12,799 --> 00:00:14,799
each  one  you  have  a  role.  You

8
00:00:14,799 --> 00:00:16,520
may  be  a  student,  a  family

9
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:18,960
member,  a  friend,  a  neighbor,  an

10
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:21,480
employee,  or  something  else.

11
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:23,279
These  roles  are  not  separate

12
00:00:23,279 --> 00:00:25,279
identities.  They  are  different  ways

13
00:00:25,279 --> 00:00:26,920
you  take  part  in  the  world  around

14
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:27,679
you.

15
00:00:27,679 --> 00:00:29,240
Understanding  them  helps  you  see

16
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:31,160
not  only  where you  belong,  but

17
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:33,000
also  how  your  actions  connect  you

18
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,160
to  other  people.  That  connection

19
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:36,879
is  the  starting  point  for  social

20
00:00:36,879 --> 00:00:37,708
awareness.

21
00:00:37,708 --> 00:00:40,068
A  role  is  simply  a  position  you

22
00:00:40,068 --> 00:00:42,228
take  in  a  certain  part  of  life.

23
00:00:42,228 --> 00:00:43,788
The  same  person  can  have  many

24
00:00:43,788 --> 00:00:45,547
roles  in  one  day.

25
00:00:45,547 --> 00:00:47,148
Someone  may  be  a  student  in  the

26
00:00:47,148 --> 00:00:49,188
morning,  a  son  or  daughter  at

27
00:00:49,188 --> 00:00:51,628
home,  a  friend  later,  and  a

28
00:00:51,628 --> 00:00:53,507
customer  at  a  shop.

29
00:00:53,507 --> 00:00:55,348
None  of  these  roles  cancels  the

30
00:00:55,348 --> 00:00:57,508
others.  They  exist  at  the  same

31
00:00:57,508 --> 00:00:59,948
time  and  each  one  changes  what  is

32
00:00:59,948 --> 00:01:02,307
expected  in  that  situation.

33
00:01:02,307 --> 00:01:04,108
This  is  why  two  people  can  live

34
00:01:04,108 --> 00:01:06,348
in  the  same  place,  but  experience

35
00:01:06,348 --> 00:01:07,747
it  differently.

36
00:01:07,747 --> 00:01:09,108
They  may  be  connected  to  different

37
00:01:09,108 --> 00:01:11,188
people,  responsibilities  and

38
00:01:11,188 --> 00:01:12,668
communities  through  the  roles  they

39
00:01:12,668 --> 00:01:13,748
have.

40
00:01:13,748 --> 00:01:16,267
A  role  is  not  just  a  label.  It

41
00:01:16,267 --> 00:01:18,124
shapes  how  you  participate.

42
00:01:18,404 --> 00:01:20,964
Here  is  the  important  part.  Roles

43
00:01:20,964 --> 00:01:23,324
usually  come  with  responsibilities.

44
00:01:23,324 --> 00:01:24,844
A  student  is  connected  to

45
00:01:24,844 --> 00:01:26,444
learning,  classmates,  and  the

46
00:01:26,444 --> 00:01:28,524
school  environment.  A  family  member

47
00:01:28,524 --> 00:01:30,044
has  responsibilities  toward  people

48
00:01:30,044 --> 00:01:32,044
at  home.  A  neighbor  shares  a

49
00:01:32,044 --> 00:01:33,723
space  with  other  people  and

50
00:01:33,723 --> 00:01:35,444
affects  that  space  through  everyday

51
00:01:35,444 --> 00:01:36,684
choices.

52
00:01:36,684 --> 00:01:38,323
Knowing  your  roles  helps  you

53
00:01:38,323 --> 00:01:40,243
notice  these  connections.  It  does

54
00:01:40,243 --> 00:01:42,163
not  mean  carrying  every  problem

55
00:01:42,163 --> 00:01:44,403
around  you.  It  means  understanding

56
00:01:44,403 --> 00:01:46,284
that  your  actions  happen  inside

57
00:01:46,284 --> 00:01:48,323
relationships  and  that  each  role

58
00:01:48,323 --> 00:01:50,284
can  affect  other  people  in  small

59
00:01:50,284 --> 00:01:52,484
or  large  ways.  This  is  where

60
00:01:52,484 --> 00:01:54,284
social  awareness  starts  to  become

61
00:01:54,284 --> 00:01:55,207
practical.

62
00:01:55,527 --> 00:01:57,487
Roles  also  help  us  understand

63
00:01:57,487 --> 00:01:59,526
communities.  A  community  can  be

64
00:01:59,526 --> 00:02:01,086
thought  of  as  people  who  share

65
00:02:01,086 --> 00:02:03,006
something  in  common  and  exist

66
00:02:03,006 --> 00:02:05,487
within  a  shared  area  or  setting.

67
00:02:05,487 --> 00:02:07,607
Think  about  a  school.  Students,

68
00:02:07,607 --> 00:02:09,607
teachers  and  staff  have  different

69
00:02:09,607 --> 00:02:12,167
roles,  but  together  they  form  one

70
00:02:12,167 --> 00:02:14,326
community.  A  family  is  another

71
00:02:14,326 --> 00:02:16,207
community.  A  hospital  can  be

72
00:02:16,207 --> 00:02:19,087
another  with  doctors,  nurses,  staff

73
00:02:19,087 --> 00:02:20,806
and  patients  connected  around  a

74
00:02:20,806 --> 00:02:23,406
shared  place  and  purpose.

75
00:02:23,406 --> 00:02:25,966
Your  roles  are  like  doors. Each

76
00:02:25,966 --> 00:02:27,286
one  connects  you  to  a  different

77
00:02:27,286 --> 00:02:29,247
community  and  each  community

78
00:02:29,247 --> 00:02:30,766
connects  you  to  different  people

79
00:02:30,766 --> 00:02:32,123
and  responsibilities.

80
00:02:32,123 --> 00:02:34,603
A  community  is  more  than  a  list

81
00:02:34,603 --> 00:02:36,723
of  people.  It  also  includes  the

82
00:02:36,723 --> 00:02:38,563
environment  and  things  that  make

83
00:02:38,563 --> 00:02:40,842
life  inside  it  possible.

84
00:02:40,842 --> 00:02:42,762
In  a  school,  that  may  include

85
00:02:42,762 --> 00:02:44,963
classrooms,  learning  materials,

86
00:02:44,963 --> 00:02:47,123
facilities,  rules,  and  shared

87
00:02:47,123 --> 00:02:48,402
spaces.

88
00:02:48,402 --> 00:02:50,203
In  a  hospital,  it  can  include

89
00:02:50,203 --> 00:02:52,723
medicine,  equipment,  rooms,  and

90
00:02:52,723 --> 00:02:55,322
systems  that  support  care.

91
00:02:55,322 --> 00:02:57,043
Looking  at  a  community  this  way

92
00:02:57,043 --> 00:02:58,523
helps  you  see  it  as  a  living

93
00:02:58,523 --> 00:02:59,642
system.

94
00:02:59,642 --> 00:03:02,923
People,  roles,  resources,  places

95
00:03:02,923 --> 00:03:04,562
and  relationships  affect  one

96
00:03:04,562 --> 00:03:05,563
another.

97
00:03:05,563 --> 00:03:07,563
When  one  part  changes,  other  parts

98
00:03:07,563 --> 00:03:08,883
of  the  community  may  feel  the

99
00:03:08,883 --> 00:03:10,163
effect.

100
00:03:10,163 --> 00:03:12,162
That  is  why  community  problems  are

101
00:03:12,162 --> 00:03:13,664
rarely  isolated.

102
00:03:13,904 --> 00:03:16,184
Communities  can  also  sit  inside

103
00:03:16,184 --> 00:03:18,584
larger  communities.  A  class  can  be

104
00:03:18,584 --> 00:03:20,264
part  of  a  school.  A  school  can

105
00:03:20,264 --> 00:03:22,464
be  part  of  an  education  system.  A

106
00:03:22,464 --> 00:03:24,183
clinic  can  be  part  of  a  hospital

107
00:03:24,183 --> 00:03:25,904
network  and  that  network  can  be

108
00:03:25,904 --> 00:03:28,223
part  of  a  wider  health  sector.  A

109
00:03:28,223 --> 00:03:30,144
neighborhood  sits  inside  a  town  or

110
00:03:30,144 --> 00:03:33,103
city  which  sits  inside  a  country.

111
00:03:33,103 --> 00:03:34,664
This  is  the  extent  of  a

112
00:03:34,664 --> 00:03:36,744
community.  The  boundary  depends  on

113
00:03:36,744 --> 00:03:37,584
what  you  were  trying  to

114
00:03:37,584 --> 00:03:39,863
understand.  Sometimes  the  useful

115
00:03:39,863 --> 00:03:42,304
view  is  very  small.  Other  times

116
00:03:42,304 --> 00:03:43,904
you  need  to  step  back  and see  the

117
00:03:43,904 --> 00:03:46,223
larger  system  around  it.  Choosing

118
00:03:46,223 --> 00:03:47,903
the  right  scale  makes  the  picture

119
00:03:47,903 --> 00:03:48,747
clearer.

120
00:03:48,747 --> 00:03:50,906
Once  you  know  which  community  you

121
00:03:50,906 --> 00:03:52,706
are  looking  at,  the  next  step  is

122
00:03:52,706 --> 00:03:54,467
to  understand  it  instead  of

123
00:03:54,467 --> 00:03:56,307
guessing  about  it.  A  simple

124
00:03:56,307 --> 00:03:58,707
investigation  can  ask  what  issue

125
00:03:58,707 --> 00:04:00,386
exists  here?  Is  it  still

126
00:04:00,386 --> 00:04:02,266
happening?  Has  anyone  tried  to

127
00:04:02,266 --> 00:04:04,586
address  it  before?  If  so,  what

128
00:04:04,586 --> 00:04:06,347
did  they  do?

129
00:04:06,347 --> 00:04:08,227
You  do  not  need  to  conduct  formal

130
00:04:08,227 --> 00:04:10,227
research  now.  Just  notice  the

131
00:04:10,227 --> 00:04:12,427
habit  behind  these  questions.  When

132
00:04:12,427 --> 00:04:13,547
you  want  to  understand  a

133
00:04:13,547 --> 00:04:16,186
community,  observation  and  reliable

134
00:04:16,186 --> 00:04:17,946
information  are  more  useful  than

135
00:04:17,946 --> 00:04:19,827
assumptions.  They  help  you  see

136
00:04:19,827 --> 00:04:21,907
what  is  actually  happening  before

137
00:04:21,907 --> 00:04:23,747
deciding  what  it  means.

138
00:04:23,747 --> 00:04:25,507
That  habit  will  matter  a  lot  in

139
00:04:25,507 --> 00:04:26,497
the  next  unit.

